<?php
declare(strict_types=1);
namespace Juzimder;

/** Renderer-neutral design guidance shared by the page and Zero Block generators. */
final class AiDesignGuidelines {
    public static function factualGranularity(): string {
        return 'Preserve the exact granularity of every supplied fact. Aggregate counts, date ranges and measurements do not establish per-item or per-location records, values, chronology, rankings or causes. Never invent a breakdown, example record or intermediate date to fill a list; omit unsupported detail and keep the supplied aggregate as an aggregate.';
    }

    public static function prompt(): string {
        return 'Before generating output, form a compact internal design spec in this same response; do not add a design-spec field. Derive purpose, audience and primary action from the owner brief. Choose each CTA only from capabilities and destinations the brief confirms. If the brief only describes browsing, reading, listening to, or viewing content, use a neutral link to an existing matching section or omit the CTA; do not promise feedback, form submission, booking, or contact unless the owner explicitly requests it and the editor supports that action. The generated page HTML has no working form or submission handler. A statement that the organization collects contributions describes its own process; do not turn it into an instruction for visitors to submit, share, register, book, request, or leave feedback. For information-only pages, use a neutral link such as “View catalogue” to an existing matching section or omit the CTA; use contact actions only when the owner supplied a real destination. Separate confirmed facts from visual choices and never present a visual choice as a business fact. Visible operational statements, contacts, destinations, schedules, prices, availability and service steps must be supported by the brief or omitted. A general mention of classes or workshops does not confirm curriculum topics, exercises, safety instructions, learning outcomes or a teaching sequence; include such details only when the owner states them, otherwise describe the lessons at the same general level as the brief. Before returning copy, audit all headings for uniqueness, preserve names and titles exactly as given, and do not invent the plot or attributes of named works or entities. Before writing, allocate each fact and list from the brief to one section. Keep the Hero focused on the page purpose; include a primary action only when the owner brief confirms a supported destination, and otherwise omit it. Put detailed facts in the single relevant body section. Never restate Hero or body facts in another section, even in different wording; repeat is repetition regardless of phrasing. In particular, do not repeat the Hero date range, place, period, category list, named items or collection scope in the next section heading or lead paragraph; use that section to add new details or a distinct perspective. An ordered list of three or more specific attributes or named items repeated in a different section is also a repeated fact; keep the list in one place and give other sections new information. If the brief provides few distinct facts, keep the page concise instead of padding or repeating content. Do not repeat the Hero’s audience or capability summary in a body section; each body section must add distinct information, and omit any section that has no new facts. Do not expand a Hero summary or list into separate body cards that repeat each listed item; a card is useful only when it adds a distinct fact or perspective. Choose one subject-specific visual idea and focal point. Define the page-wide visual system once: reuse the same canvas, surface, ink, muted-text and accent roles; heading/body typography roles; a restrained spacing scale; and consistent radii. Derive these values from validated project settings when present, otherwise from the visual idea. If the design spec names a color family or light/dark palette, translate it into the page-wide color roles and keep it consistent; do not substitute an unrelated dominant hue. Reuse the system across sections while allowing their layouts to differ. Across the page, keep a stable role hierarchy: one dominant page title, a consistent section-heading style, subordinate card titles, quieter supporting copy and compact badges; vary each section layout when its content benefits. Give every content block a compact pill badge sized to its label. Align section containers to the Hero’s left edge when the Hero copy is left-aligned. When the page uses rounded cards or buttons, match photo corner radii to that same radius. Keep image credits as text and links at 8px or smaller; place credits below inline photos, and discreetly inside the block for background photos without disrupting its composition. When generating multiple sections, choose each layout for its content and vary neighboring layouts instead of repeating one template. Treat composition as a content decision, not decoration. Map sections by information role: use editorial prose for context, ordered steps only for a real sequence, a directory or list for entries, a gallery for visual works, a comparison only for supported comparable facts, and an equal card grid only for genuinely peer items. Across multiple body sections, use at least two different structures when the content supports them; do not use card grids for every section or turn every paragraph into a card. Variation should be noticeable in structure, not only background color. The presence of an image slot alone must not force a split Hero. Choose the Hero composition by function, not habit: use typography-led composition when the subject is abstract or a relevant photo adds little; use a split composition when a contextual photo supports the message but should stay beside the copy; use a full-bleed background only when a genuinely relevant scene can act as the focal point and the copy remains readable over it. A requested photo slot does not guarantee a suitable photo will be found, so keep a deliberate no-photo composition and never force a photo layout. For any icon, choose one clearly relevant to its nearby content; color it with the page’s existing ink or accent role, and omit generic decoration. Use only one icon family per page: Tabler Outline; never mix icon libraries, weights or filled styles. Use openly licensed photos only when their actual subject matches; omit weak matches and preserve required attribution. Adapt the composition within the active renderer’s geometry, responsive model and existing response schema. Priority: explicit owner requirements and validated project settings, then applicable user Skill requirements, then accessible defaults; all remain subordinate to safety and factual accuracy.';
    }
}
